"""Interview question bank: web, frontend, backend frameworks, and databases."""

QUESTIONS = {
    # Web fundamentals
    "HTML": [
        ("easy", "What is the difference between block-level and inline elements?",
         "Block elements start a new line and take the full available width, like div and p. Inline elements flow within text and only take the space they need, like span and a."),
        ("easy", "What does the `required` attribute do on an input?",
         "It marks the field as mandatory, and the browser blocks form submission until it has a value, while also exposing the required state to CSS and assistive technology."),
        ("medium", "Explain semantic HTML and why it matters for accessibility and SEO.",
         "Semantic elements like header, nav, main, article, section, and aside convey meaning to browsers and screen readers, replacing generic div soup. Search engines and assistive tech use the document outline to navigate, so semantics improve both."),
        ("medium", "What are the differences between localStorage, sessionStorage, and cookies?",
         "localStorage persists until cleared and is shared across tabs, sessionStorage is per-tab and cleared on close, and cookies are sent with every request and have size and expiry limits. Never put tokens in localStorage if XSS is a concern."),
        ("hard", "Explain content security policy, XSS, and how to defend against injection.",
         "CSP restricts which script, style, and frame sources the browser will execute, limiting XSS damage even if injection occurs. Prevent XSS by escaping output, using textContent instead of innerHTML, sanitizing with a vetted library, and deploying a strict nonce- or hash-based CSP."),
    ],
    "CSS": [
        ("easy", "What is the box model and what do box-sizing values change?",
         "Content, padding, border, and margin make up the box. With content-box, width applies only to content; with border-box, width includes padding and border, which is usually what you want."),
        ("easy", "What is the difference between `margin` and `padding`?",
         "Padding is space inside the border around content, while margin is space outside the border between elements. Margins collapse between siblings, padding never does."),
        ("medium", "Explain the difference between position values static, relative, absolute, fixed, and sticky.",
         "Static is the default flow position, relative offsets without leaving flow, absolute anchors to the nearest positioned ancestor, fixed anchors to the viewport, and sticky toggles between relative and fixed based on scroll position."),
        ("medium", "What are CSS custom properties and why are they useful?",
         "Variables declared with --name inherit through the DOM and can be changed at runtime by toggling a class or style attribute. This makes theming and design tokens trivial and avoids repeating literals."),
        ("hard", "Explain CSS specificity, the cascade, and how to avoid specificity wars.",
         "The cascade resolves conflicts by origin, importance, layer, specificity, then source order, so a utility class with higher specificity can beat a component style. Keep specificity flat with layers, :where(), low-specificity resets, and BEM or utility classes instead of long descendant selectors."),
    ],
    # Frontend frameworks
    "React.js": [
        ("easy", "What is state and how do you update it correctly?",
         "State is component-owned data that triggers re-render when changed. Always update with the setter using a functional form, setCount(c => c + 1), because direct mutation does not trigger re-render and stale closures break batching."),
        ("easy", "What is the difference between props and state?",
         "Props are read-only data passed down from a parent, while state is local and owned by the component. A component re-renders when either changes."),
        ("medium", "Explain how the React reconciliation algorithm and keys work.",
         "React builds a virtual DOM and diffs old and new trees to apply minimal changes, matching siblings by key. Stable unique keys let the algorithm move rather than recreate nodes; using array index as a key causes state bugs on reorder."),
        ("medium", "What is the difference between useEffect and useLayoutEffect, and what causes infinite re-renders?",
         "useLayoutEffect runs synchronously after DOM mutation before paint, needed for measuring layout. useEffect runs after paint. A render that sets state which triggers another render, such as an unguarded fetch in the body, causes an infinite loop; use functional updates with a guard or a ref."),
        ("hard", "Explain React 18 concurrent features: transitions, Suspense, and useTransition.",
         "useTransition marks state updates as non-urgent so typing stays responsive while heavy re-renders proceed in the background. Suspense pauses rendering for lazy components or data fetching, showing a fallback, and transitions prevent tearing during concurrent renders."),
    ],
    "Next.js": [
        ("easy", "What is server-side rendering and why does it help performance?",
         "SSR renders HTML on the server per request, so users see content immediately and crawlers can index it. It costs extra server work and can slow time-to-interactive if hydration is heavy."),
        ("easy", "What is the difference between the `pages` and `app` routers?",
         "The pages router uses file-based routing with getServerSideProps and getStaticProps, while the app router uses layouts, server components by default, and streaming with Suspense."),
        ("medium", "What are Server Components and how do they differ from Client Components?",
         "Server Components render only on the server, can access Node APIs and secrets, and ship zero JavaScript. Client Components are marked with 'use client', hydrate in the browser, and are needed for hooks, state, and event handlers."),
        ("medium", "Explain static generation, ISR, and dynamic rendering in Next.js.",
         "Static generation builds HTML at build time, ISR revalidates pages on a time or on-demand trigger, and dynamic rendering runs per request for request-specific data. Choose the least dynamic option the page needs for the best cache behavior."),
        ("hard", "How do you optimize data fetching to avoid waterfalls in the App Router?",
         "Parallelize with Promise.all or the top-level await pattern, fetch in layouts or parallel route segments to share data, and use Suspense streaming so slow sections do not block. Cache aggressively with the fetch cache and revalidateTag, and colocate data fetching in the segment that needs it."),
    ],
    "Angular": [
        ("easy", "What is a component and what are its key parts?",
         "A component has a template, styles, and a class. The class holds properties, the constructor injection, and lifecycle hooks like ngOnInit and ngOnDestroy."),
        ("easy", "What is the difference between a component and a directive?",
         "A component is a directive with a template that renders a view. A structural directive such as ngIf changes DOM structure, and an attribute directive such as ngClass only changes an element's behavior or look."),
        ("medium", "Explain dependency injection in Angular and the role of Injectable.",
         "The framework constructs dependencies and injects them through the constructor or inject() function, using a hierarchical injector tree. Services marked @Injectable({providedIn: 'root'}) are singletons available application-wide."),
        ("medium", "What is change detection and how does OnPush differ from the default strategy?",
         "Default change detection checks the whole component tree on every event. OnPush only checks a component when its inputs change, an event fires inside it, or you call markForCheck, which requires immutable inputs and greatly improves performance."),
        ("hard", "Explain RxJS operators and how to avoid memory leaks with async pipe and takeUntilDestroyed.",
         "RxJS streams are lazy, cold, and composable via operators like map, switchMap, and catchError. Subscribing manually without unsubscribing leaks memory and keeps change detection running, so use the async pipe in templates or call takeUntilDestroyed with a DestroyRef in components."),
    ],
    "Vue.js": [
        ("easy", "What are the lifecycle hooks in Vue 3 and when is data fetched?",
         "The order is onBeforeMount, onMounted, onUpdated, onBeforeUnmount, onUnmounted, plus onBeforeUpdate and onErrorCaptured. Fetch data in onMounted or in a setup composable, not before mount, when the DOM is needed."),
        ("easy", "What is the difference between ref and reactive in Vue 3?",
         "ref wraps a value in an object with a .value property and works for primitives, while reactive returns a proxy for objects. In templates and JS, refs are auto-unwrapped at the top level, so prefer ref for most state."),
        ("medium", "Explain the Composition API and why composables help.",
         "The Composition API groups logic by feature instead of by option type, and composables are functions that encapsulate and reuse stateful logic. Each call to a composable gets its own isolated state, unlike a plain mixin that shares and collides."),
        ("medium", "How does Vue's reactivity work and what causes reactivity to be lost?",
         "Vue 3 uses ES Proxy with dependency tracking and triggers, so nested property access is tracked. Reactivity is lost when you destructure a reactive object, because you break the link, so use toRefs to keep per-property reactivity or keep the object intact."),
        ("hard", "Explain Vue's virtual DOM diffing, keys, and how to avoid unnecessary re-renders.",
         "Vue patches the vdom using sameVnode type and key comparisons, so stable keys prevent full re-creation on reorder. Mark static subtrees with v-once or v-memo, keep props stable, and move logic into computed properties so the render function stays pure."),
    ],
    "Svelte": [
        ("easy", "What makes Svelte different from React and Vue?",
         "Svelte compiles to imperative DOM-manipulation code at build time, so there is no virtual DOM diff at runtime and no runtime library shipped to the browser. Components are reactive by assignment syntax rather than hooks."),
        ("easy", "What is Svelte's reactive statement syntax?",
         "A reactive declaration uses the $: label, and it re-runs when any referenced reactive variable changes. Assignments in event handlers or functions trigger updates automatically."),
        ("medium", "Explain Svelte stores and how they differ from component state.",
         "A store is an object with subscribe, set, and update, created with writable or readable. Stores are shared across components, and the $store auto-subscription syntax subscribes in the template and unsubscribes on destroy."),
        ("medium", "What is the difference between SvelteKit load functions and actions?",
         "A load function runs on server and client to fetch data for a route, and form actions handle form submissions. Actions return a fail or success result that populates form state, replacing manual fetch and error handling."),
        ("hard", "How does Svelte 5 runes system change reactivity compared to Svelte 4?",
         "Runes, $state, $derived, and $effect, make dependencies explicit at the call site instead of lexical, and work outside components in shared modules. Effects run only in the browser after mount, they batch updates with scheduling, and deep reactivity uses proxies for objects and a copy-on-write proxy for plain values."),
    ],
    # State management
    "Redux": [
        ("easy", "What are the three core Redux principles?",
         "There is a single store, state is read-only and only changed by dispatch, and reducers are pure functions that return new state instead of mutating."),
        ("easy", "What is an action, and what shape does it usually take?",
         "An action is a plain object with a type field and optional payload, describing what happened. Type is usually a unique string constant and payload carries the data."),
        ("medium", "Explain how middleware extends Redux and why it matters for side effects.",
         "Middleware wraps dispatch, so it can log, persist, or handle async before the action reaches reducers. Thunk middleware lets you dispatch functions returning promises, and redux-saga or RTK Query manage async flows declaratively."),
        ("medium", "What is the difference between slice reducers and extraReducers in Redux Toolkit?",
         "A slice reducer handles actions it generates with createSlice, while extraReducers reacts to actions defined elsewhere using builder.addMatcher or addCase. This keeps store setup concise with Immer-immutable updates."),
        ("hard", "Explain Redux performance pitfalls: unnecessary re-renders and selector design.",
         "Any store change notifies all subscribers, so components re-render unless a selector returns an equal value. Use createSelector for memoization, avoid returning new object or array literals from selectors, and split frequently changing state from rarely changing state."),
    ],
    # API design
    "GraphQL": [
        ("easy", "What problem does GraphQL solve compared to REST?",
         "Clients request exactly the fields they need in a single round trip, avoiding over-fetching and under-fetching. The server exposes a typed schema instead of many fixed endpoints."),
        ("easy", "What is a GraphQL resolver?",
         "A resolver is a function that returns the value for a field of a type. Field resolvers on the default resolver read the property, and custom resolvers fetch related data for nested selections."),
        ("medium", "Explain N+1 queries in GraphQL and how to solve them.",
         "A list of users each fetching their posts issues one query per parent, because resolvers run per field. Fix with DataLoader batching per request, so all posts are fetched in a single query and cached for the request."),
        ("medium", "What are GraphQL mutations and why are they different from queries?",
         "Mutations modify state and execute serially, one at a time, while queries may run in parallel. Their input types define what can be written, and they should be idempotent or return errors explicitly."),
        ("hard", "Explain schema stitching, federation, and query complexity limits.",
         "Federation composes separately owned services by making each subgraph's types extendable, so the gateway merges the schemas. Because a client can nest arbitrarily, you need depth and cost limits, persisted queries, timeouts, and disabled introspection in production."),
    ],
    "REST API": [
        ("easy", "What are the main HTTP methods and their semantics?",
         "GET reads and is safe, POST creates and is not idempotent, PUT replaces and is idempotent, PATCH partially updates, and DELETE removes. GET must never change state."),
        ("easy", "What status codes are most commonly used and what do they mean?",
         "200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content, 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized, 403 Forbidden, 404 Not Found, 409 Conflict, 422 Unprocessable, 429 Too Many Requests, and 500 Server Error."),
        ("medium", "How do you version a REST API and what are the tradeoffs?",
         "URI versioning like /v1/resources is visible and easy to route, while header versioning like Accept: application/vnd.api+1 is cleaner. Version only when you must, prefer additive backward-compatible changes, and support a sunset date for deprecated versions."),
        ("medium", "How do you implement pagination, filtering, and sorting consistently?",
         "Use cursor pagination for large or changing datasets, offset for small static ones, and standard query params like limit, offset, sort, and filter. Return a meta object with total, next cursor, and has_more so clients can paginate reliably."),
        ("hard", "How do you design idempotency and handle partial failures in distributed API calls?",
         "Accept an Idempotency-Key header, store the key with the response, and replay the stored result on retry so a timeout never double-charges. For multi-step operations use sagas with compensating actions instead of distributed transactions, and return partial results with per-item status."),
    ],
    "RESTful Services": [
        ("easy", "What does stateless mean in the context of REST?",
         "Each request contains everything the server needs, so the server keeps no client session between requests. This enables horizontal scaling and caching at any layer."),
        ("easy", "What are the constraints of a REST architecture?",
         "Client-server separation, statelessness, cacheability, uniform interface, layered system, and optional code-on-demand. Together they produce scalability and loose coupling."),
        ("medium", "How do you design RESTful resources and URIs?",
         "Model nouns not verbs, use plural collections like /users, nest for ownership like /users/1/orders, and use HTTP verbs for actions. Keep URIs stable and put side effects in POST sub-resources like /orders/1/refund when a verb is unavoidable."),
        ("medium", "How do you handle caching with ETag and Cache-Control?",
         "ETag is a version fingerprint of the representation, and a conditional GET returns 304 Not Modified if unchanged. Cache-Control sets freshness like max-age, plus no-store for sensitive data, and Vary tells caches which headers affect the response."),
        ("hard", "Explain content negotiation, hypermedia (HATEOAS), and the Richardson maturity model.",
         "Content negotiation returns the representation matching Accept, Accept-Language, or custom media types. HATEOAS embeds links to valid next actions so clients discover behavior. The maturity model ranks services from level 0 chaos up through resources, verbs, hypermedia, and code-on-demand."),
    ],
    # Backend frameworks
    "Node.js": [
        ("easy", "What is the event loop and why does it matter for Node.js?",
         "Node runs JavaScript on a single thread with a libuv thread pool for I/O, and the event loop dispatches callbacks once the stack is empty. CPU-heavy work blocks the loop, so offload it to workers or child processes."),
        ("easy", "What is the difference between process.env and dotenv?",
         "process.env reads environment variables the OS provides, while dotenv loads a .env file into process.env for local development. Never commit the .env file."),
        ("medium", "How do you avoid callback hell in Node.js?",
         "Use promises with async/await for sequencing, Promise.all for parallel independent work, and async iterators or streams for large data. Promises give a single error propagation path instead of nested error-first callbacks."),
        ("medium", "Explain streams and backpressure in Node.js.",
         "A stream processes data incrementally instead of buffering it all. When the writable side is slower than the readable side, backpressure propagates so the producer pauses via the return value of write() or pipe()."),
        ("hard", "How do you diagnose and fix a memory leak or event-loop lag in a Node.js service?",
         "Profile with heap snapshots and --inspect to find retained objects, check for unremoved listeners, unbounded caches, and missing stream cleanup. For event-loop lag, profile CPU per tick, and move blocking work to worker_threads so the loop stays responsive."),
    ],
    "Express.js": [
        ("easy", "What is middleware in Express and how does the chain work?",
         "Middleware are functions with (req, res, next) that run in order for each request, and next() passes control onward. Route handlers and error handlers are middleware with specific roles."),
        ("easy", "How do you define route parameters and query strings?",
         "Named parameters use a colon, such as /users/:id and /users/:id/posts, and are read from req.params. Query strings come from req.query, and the body from req.body with a JSON body parser."),
        ("medium", "How does error handling work in Express, including async errors?",
         "Error-handling middleware has four parameters (err, req, res, next) and is mounted last, so it catches errors from earlier middleware. Since Express 4 does not catch rejected promises automatically, wrap async handlers or use a wrapper like express-async-errors."),
        ("medium", "What is the difference between middlewares order like helmet, cors, and express.json, and why does order matter?",
         "Security and parsing middleware should run before routes, and CORS headers must be set before the response is sent. Because middleware runs sequentially, placing auth after a public route handler would expose that route."),
        ("hard", "How do you structure a large Express app for maintainability and graceful shutdown?",
         "Split into routers, controllers, services, and repositories, with dependency injection through a small container instead of globals. Handle SIGTERM by stopping new requests, letting in-flight ones finish with a timeout, closing the database pool, and flushing logs."),
    ],
    "FastAPI": [
        ("easy", "How do you define and validate a request body in FastAPI?",
         "Declare a Pydantic model as a function parameter, and FastAPI parses, validates, coerces types, and documents it in OpenAPI automatically. Type hints drive the whole contract."),
        ("easy", "What are path, query, and body parameters in FastAPI?",
         "Path parameters are in the URL like /items/{item_id} and declared in the path string. Query parameters are function arguments that are not path or body, and the body is a Pydantic model."),
        ("medium", "Explain dependency injection in FastAPI and yield-based dependencies.",
         "Depends declares reusable dependencies that FastAPI resolves and caches per request, and sub-dependencies compose. A dependency that yields acts as a context manager, so database sessions close and resources release after the response."),
        ("medium", "How do you handle async versus sync endpoints in FastAPI?",
         "Define `async def` endpoints to await I/O directly, and plain `def` endpoints run in a threadpool so blocking libraries do not stall the loop. Mixing them wrongly is a common source of bottlenecks."),
        ("hard", "How would you scale a FastAPI service: async I/O, horizontal scaling, caching, and background work?",
         "Use async handlers plus async DB drivers for I/O throughput, stateless instances behind a load balancer with a shared cache like Redis. Add response and dependency caching for hot reads, and move slow work to a task queue with background workers so requests return immediately."),
    ],
    "Django": [
        ("easy", "What is the Django ORM and how do you define a model?",
         "Models are Python classes subclassing models.Model whose fields become database columns. The ORM generates SQL, handles migrations, and lets you query with chained, lazy lookups."),
        ("easy", "What is the difference between a QuerySet and a list in Django?",
         "A QuerySet is lazy, immutable, and chainable, and it is evaluated only when iterated, sliced, or converted to a list. Reusing a QuerySet caches its result, so call refresh() or re-query to get fresh data."),
        ("medium", "Explain Django middleware and the request/response lifecycle.",
         "The request passes through middleware in order, reaches the URL resolver, then the view, and the response travels back out. Middleware can modify the request or short-circuit with a response, which is where auth and logging usually live."),
        ("medium", "What is select_related and prefetch_related, and what problem do they solve?",
         "select_related joins in a single SQL query for ForeignKey and OneToOne, while prefetch_related runs a second query and stitches results in Python for reverse and many-to-many relations. They eliminate N+1 query problems."),
        ("hard", "How do you secure a Django app: CSRF, auth, and query safety?",
         "Keep CsrfViewMiddleware on and use {% csrf_token %} in templates, store passwords with Django's hasher, and use permission classes per view. Never interpolate raw user input into raw SQL; use filter or raw with parameters, and run check --deploy before shipping."),
    ],
    "Flask": [
        ("easy", "What is a Flask route and how do you bind a URL variable?",
         "A route decorator maps a URL rule to a function, and a converter in angle brackets captures a variable, as in @app.route('/user/<int:user_id>'). The value arrives as a function argument with the type already coerced."),
        ("easy", "What is a Flask application context and why does it matter?",
         "The app context binds current_app, g, and the database session for the request. Without it, code outside a request, like a CLI script or background task, cannot resolve app resources."),
        ("medium", "Explain the Flask extension pattern and application factory.",
         "Extensions like Flask-SQLAlchemy are objects initialized with the app in an application factory that returns the configured app. This avoids circular imports and makes testing with multiple configs straightforward."),
        ("medium", "How do you structure error handling and configuration in Flask?",
         "Register errorhandler for HTTPException and custom exceptions, and load config from environment variables or a config object with a class hierarchy. Keep secrets out of code and validate configuration at startup so misconfigurations fail fast."),
        ("hard", "How do you make a Flask app production-ready for concurrency and scaling?",
         "Run it under a production WSGI server like gunicorn with multiple workers, use a real database connection pool, and set proxy headers so scheme and host are correct. Cache expensive computations, run long tasks in a queue, and make the app stateless for horizontal scaling."),
    ],
    "Spring Boot": [
        ("easy", "What is dependency injection in Spring and what is a bean?",
         "A bean is an object managed by the IoC container whose lifecycle the framework controls. Dependencies are injected through constructor injection, which is preferred because it keeps objects immutable and testable."),
        ("easy", "What does @Autowired do and why prefer constructor injection?",
         "@Autowired asks the container to resolve and inject a dependency. Constructor injection makes dependencies explicit, supports final fields, fails fast if unsatisfied, and simplifies unit tests without reflection or mocks in fields."),
        ("medium", "Explain Spring's request lifecycle and the DispatcherServlet.",
         "A request hits DispatcherServlet, which consults HandlerMappings to find the controller, invokes it with HandlerMethodArgumentResolvers and interceptors, then renders the return value through HttpMessageConverters. Exception handling resolves through HandlerExceptionResolvers."),
        ("medium", "What is the difference between @Transactional propagation and isolation levels?",
         "Propagation defines what happens when a transactional method is called inside another transaction, such as REQUIRED joining it, REQUIRES_NEW suspending it, or NESTED using a savepoint. Isolation sets the visibility of concurrent transactions, from READ_UNCOMMITTED to SERIALIZABLE."),
        ("hard", "How do you profile and optimize a slow Spring Boot application?",
         "Use Actuator endpoints for health and metrics, add Micrometer timers around suspect paths, and take thread dumps plus a JFR recording to find contention and GC pauses. Fix root causes such as N+1 queries with fetch joins, add caching with @Cacheable, and move blocking IO off request threads with async or reactive endpoints."),
    ],
    ".NET": [
        ("easy", "What is the difference between a .NET application and .NET Framework?",
         ".NET is the modern cross-platform runtime with .NET Core lineage, while .NET Framework is Windows-only and legacy. .NET 5+ unified them into a single .NET version scheme."),
        ("easy", "What is garbage collection in .NET and when is it triggered?",
         "The GC runs automatically when memory pressure builds or a Gen0 allocation budget is exceeded, reclaiming unreachable objects. You can force a collection with GC.Collect but should almost never need to."),
        ("medium", "Explain the difference between async, await, and Task in C#.",
         "Task represents an asynchronous operation, and async methods return Task or Task<T>. await suspends without blocking a thread, resuming on the captured context unless ConfigureAwait(false) is used, which is essential in libraries to avoid deadlocks."),
        ("medium", "How do you work with configuration and dependency injection in ASP.NET Core?",
         "Configuration is layered from appsettings.json, environment variables, and command line through the IConfiguration interface with IOptions pattern for typed access. Services are registered in the container with lifetimes such as singleton, scoped, and transient via AddScoped and resolved by constructor injection."),
        ("hard", "How do you diagnose and improve ASP.NET Core performance under load?",
         "Measure with dotnet-counters, Application Insights, and BenchmarkDotNet for micro-benchmarks, then look at thread pool starvation, allocation pressure driving Gen2 collections, and blocking calls on request threads. Enable response compression and caching, pool HTTP clients with IHttpClientFactory, and use asynchronous streaming for large payloads."),
    ],
    "Laravel": [
        ("easy", "What is an Eloquent model and how do you define a relationship?",
         "An Eloquent model maps to a database table and adds hydration, query building, and events. Define relations with methods like hasMany, belongsTo, and belongsToMany so the query builder can join data."),
        ("easy", "What are migrations and why are they used?",
         "Migrations are versioned PHP files describing schema changes, so schema changes are reproducible across environments. Run them with php artisan migrate and roll back with migrate:rollback."),
        ("medium", "Explain the service container and dependency injection in Laravel.",
         "The container binds interfaces to implementations, resolves constructor type hints automatically, and supports contextual binding and facades. Bind in a service provider and type-hint the interface for testability."),
        ("medium", "What are queues and jobs in Laravel, and how are they dispatched?",
         "Jobs are queued units of work processed by workers, offloading slow tasks like email or image processing from the request. Dispatch with dispatch(new SendEmailJob) or Bus::dispatch and configure the queue connection, retry, and timeout in config or the job class."),
        ("hard", "How do you prevent N+1 queries and optimize heavy Eloquent reports?",
         "Eager load with with, use withCount for aggregates, and select only needed columns to avoid hydrating full models. For large data use chunkById or lazyById, and cache computed read models rather than querying per row in a loop."),
    ],
    "Tailwind CSS": [
        ("easy", "What is the utility-first approach and how does it differ from component CSS?",
         "Instead of writing custom class rules, you compose small single-purpose utilities in markup, like flex items-center gap-2. Design decisions live in the markup and reuse comes from the constrained utility set rather than a stylesheet."),
        ("easy", "How do you customize the Tailwind theme?",
         "Extend theme in the config with colors, spacing, and fonts, or add CSS variables in newer versions so utilities like bg-brand can reference them. Variants like hover and dark are composed with prefixes."),
        ("medium", "How does the @apply directive work and when is it a bad idea?",
         "@apply inlines existing utilities into a custom class, useful for frequently repeated patterns like btn. It is bad in most cases because it duplicates CSS and breaks utility semantics, so prefer a component in a framework layer or a plain CSS class."),
        ("medium", "What is the difference between the JIT engine and the classic engine?",
         "JIT scans templates and generates only the utilities used, producing far smaller CSS and enabling arbitrary values like w-[37px]. The classic engine shipped the full utility set, which is why JIT became the default."),
        ("hard", "How do you control CSS size and avoid class bloat in production?",
         "Rely on JIT content scanning of real source files, so avoid dynamically constructed class names the scanner cannot see. Extract shared patterns into component classes, purge unused files from the content config, and monitor built CSS size in CI to catch regressions."),
    ],
    "Bootstrap": [
        ("easy", "What is the Bootstrap grid system?",
         "A 12-column flexbox grid with breakpoints xs, sm, md, lg, xl, and xxl. Columns use col classes and responsive variants like col-md-6, plus gutters controlled with g-*."),
        ("easy", "What are Bootstrap components?",
         "Prebuilt reusable UI pieces like navbar, dropdown, modal, carousel, and alert. They are styled, responsive, and accessible out of the box."),
        ("medium", "How does responsive utility work in Bootstrap?",
         "Utilities are prefixed with a breakpoint, such as d-none d-md-block, meaning hidden on small screens and shown from md up. Classes cascade by breakpoint, so utilities override in ascending order of screen size."),
        ("medium", "How do you customize Bootstrap with Sass variables?",
         "Override the Sass variables before importing Bootstrap, such as $primary and $body-bg, then import only the modules you need to keep CSS small. In v5.3, CSS variables let you theme components without recompiling Sass."),
        ("hard", "How do you build an accessible modal or dropdown with Bootstrap and avoid common pitfalls?",
         "Bootstrap handles focus trapping and ARIA attributes, but you must not nest modals, must set aria-labelledby and aria-labelledby targets, and must not hide content from keyboard users with display none. Ensure focus returns to the trigger on close and that backdrop scroll is disabled without layout shift."),
    ],
    # Databases
    "SQL": [
        ("easy", "What is the difference between WHERE and HAVING?",
         "WHERE filters rows before grouping and cannot use aggregate functions. HAVING filters groups after GROUP BY and accepts aggregate results like COUNT(*) > 5."),
        ("easy", "Explain INNER JOIN, LEFT JOIN, RIGHT JOIN, and FULL OUTER JOIN.",
         "INNER returns only matching rows, LEFT keeps all left rows with NULLs for unmatched right, RIGHT is the mirror, and FULL OUTER keeps both sides with NULLs where unmatched."),
        ("medium", "What are the ACID properties and what does isolation level control?",
         "Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability. Isolation levels trade anomalies for concurrency, from READ UNCOMMITTED up through READ COMMITTED, REPEATABLE READ, and SERIALIZABLE, with the Anomaly view of SQL92."),
        ("medium", "How do indexes work and what are the types of indexes?",
         "B-tree indexes keep data ordered for fast range and equality lookups, hash indexes optimize exact match, and bitmap indexes help low-cardinality columns. Partial, covering, composite, and expression indexes tailor the structure to your query patterns."),
        ("hard", "How do you diagnose and fix a slow query using EXPLAIN and execution plans?",
         "Read the plan for the expensive node, estimate versus actual row counts, and scan type. Fix with correct composite index column order, covering indexes to avoid lookups, rewriting the predicate, and removing N+1 patterns, then re-check the plan with ANALYZE."),
    ],
    "PostgreSQL": [
        ("easy", "How do you create a table and add a primary key?",
         "Use CREATE TABLE with column types and PRIMARY KEY, or SERIAL plus PRIMARY KEY for auto-increment. Use IDENTITY in modern versions since SERIAL is a legacy pseudo-type."),
        ("easy", "What is a schema in PostgreSQL?",
         "A schema is a namespace for tables, views, and functions, letting multiple teams share a database without name collisions. search_path controls which schemas are searched."),
        ("medium", "Explain MVCC and how PostgreSQL handles concurrent updates.",
         "MVCC keeps multiple row versions so readers never block writers and writers never block readers. Each transaction sees a consistent snapshot, and vacuuming removes dead tuples and updates visibility maps."),
        ("medium", "What are indexes in PostgreSQL and which types are available?",
         "B-tree for equality and ranges, GIN for arrays and full-text and jsonb, GiST for geometric and range types, and BRIN for very large naturally ordered tables. Partial and expression indexes reduce size and cost."),
        ("hard", "How would you diagnose slow queries with EXPLAIN ANALYZE and fix them in a production database?",
         "Run EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) to compare estimated and actual rows and see buffer hits versus reads, which reveals stale statistics and missing indexes. Fix with VACUUM ANALYZE for stats, targeted indexes, query rewrites, and partitioning for huge tables, all while watching lock impact and statement_timeout."),
    ],
    "MySQL": [
        ("easy", "What are the main differences between MySQL and PostgreSQL?",
         "MySQL is optimized for read-heavy web workloads with simple replication, while PostgreSQL has stronger SQL compliance, richer types like JSONB and arrays, and better concurrency. InnoDB supports transactions and foreign keys in both, but PG enforces more strictly."),
        ("easy", "How do you see which queries MySQL is running?",
         "Use SHOW PROCESSLIST, or the performance_schema and sys schema views such as statements_with_sorting and schema_unused_indexes. Enable the slow query log with long_query_time to catch offenders."),
        ("medium", "Explain InnoDB's clustering and how primary key choice affects performance.",
         "InnoDB stores rows in tablespace order by the primary key, so the primary key is the physical row order. A random or wide primary key, like a UUID, causes page splits and fragmentation, so prefer a monotonic auto-increment or ordered key."),
        ("medium", "What are the transaction isolation levels in MySQL?",
         "READ UNCOMMITTED, READ COMMITTED, REPEATABLE READ (default, with gap locking preventing phantoms), and SERIALIZABLE. MVCC implements the first three with undo logs, and SERIALIZABLE adds full locking."),
        ("hard", "How do you fix MySQL replication lag and slow queries at scale?",
         "For lag, check replica IO and SQL threads, avoid single-threaded bottlenecks by using parallel replication, and reduce row-change volume on the master. For slow queries use EXPLAIN ANALYZE, add composite indexes, avoid SELECT *, enable the slow log, and shard or archive cold data."),
    ],
    "MongoDB": [
        ("easy", "What is a document and how does it differ from a row?",
         "MongoDB stores flexible BSON documents, so fields can be arrays or nested objects and schema is optional. Documents avoid joins and mapping, making reads single-round-trip."),
        ("easy", "What are the CRUD operations in MongoDB?",
         "insertOne and insertMany, find and findOne, updateOne, updateMany with update operators, replaceOne, and deleteOne or deleteMany. Aggregations use the aggregation pipeline for transforms."),
        ("medium", "Explain indexes in MongoDB and how compound index order matters.",
         "MongoDB supports single-field, compound, multikey, text, and geospatial indexes. In a compound index the order of fields decides which prefixes are usable, so put equality-matched fields first and range fields last."),
        ("medium", "What is the aggregation pipeline and what are the most used stages?",
         "A pipeline is a stream of documents transformed by stages: $match filters early, $project reshapes, $group aggregates, $unwind flattens arrays, $lookup joins, and $sort plus $limit page. Put $match first to reduce work."),
        ("hard", "Explain read/write concerns, transactions, and how to diagnose slow MongoDB queries.",
         "Read concern governs visibility, and w:1 vs w:majority trades consistency for latency, while readPreference offloads to secondaries. Multi-document transactions are expensive because of locking, so prefer embedding over joining. Diagnose with explain('executionStats'), the profiler, and checking for collection scans without indexes."),
    ],
    "SQLite": [
        ("easy", "What makes SQLite different from other SQL databases?",
         "SQLite is serverless and embedded: the whole database is a single file with no separate process. It is ideal for local, mobile, and testing use, with limited concurrent writes."),
        ("easy", "How do you enable write-ahead logging and why?",
         "Run PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL so readers do not block the writer and vice versa, which greatly improves concurrent read performance. Remember to set busy_timeout when contention is possible."),
        ("medium", "Explain the SQLite type system and the use of the rowid.",
         "SQLite uses dynamic typing with type affinity, so the column type guides conversion rather than being strictly enforced. Every table has an implicit rowid unless declared WITHOUT ROWID, which can improve locality and storage."),
        ("medium", "How do transactions work in SQLite and what are the locking modes?",
         "A transaction wraps work for atomicity and durability, and a failure rolls back all changes. Readers block writers in rollback-journal mode, while WAL allows one writer with many concurrent readers."),
        ("hard", "How do you use SQLite in production for an app with moderate traffic?",
         "Use WAL mode with a busy timeout, keep the database on local SSD storage, and pool connections per thread since the library is not thread-safe by default. Set synchronous to NORMAL for a durability trade-off, checkpoint regularly, and migrate to a client/server database once you need multiple writers across machines."),
    ],
    "Redis": [
        ("easy", "What is Redis and what is it commonly used for?",
         "Redis is an in-memory key-value store used for caching, session storage, rate limiting, queues, and pub/sub. It supports strings, hashes, lists, sets, sorted sets, and streams with optional persistence."),
        ("easy", "What is the difference between a cache-aside pattern and write-through?",
         "Cache-aside reads from the cache and falls back to the database, populating on miss, so the application owns logic. Write-through updates cache and database together, so data never goes stale but writes are slower."),
        ("medium", "What are Redis eviction policies and the difference between maxmemory and TTL?",
         "When maxmemory is hit, Redis evicts per policy: allkeys-lru, allkeys-random, or volatile-lru which only removes keys with TTL. TTL expires keys by age, and combining both protects memory and keeps hot data."),
        ("medium", "Explain Redis persistence: RDB snapshots and AOF logs.",
         "RDB writes a compact point-in-time binary snapshot at intervals, fast to load but can lose recent writes. AOF appends every write command, offering better durability at a cost in size and replay time, and you can combine both."),
        ("hard", "How do you prevent cache stampedes and hot key problems in Redis?",
         "Stampedes: use a short-TTL jitter, request coalescing with a lock or single-flight, and serve stale while revalidating. Hot keys: add local in-process caches, replicate the key, shard by key, or precompute and refresh in the background rather than on read."),
    ],
    "ClickHouse": [
        ("easy", "What is ClickHouse and what workloads is it designed for?",
         "ClickHouse is a column-oriented OLAP database optimized for aggregations over large event tables. Columnar storage reads only the needed columns, and merge tree parts give fast compression and scans."),
        ("easy", "What is the difference between ORDER BY and PARTITION BY?",
         "PARTITION BY splits data into separate parts, usually by coarse keys like month, which prunes whole parts. ORDER BY sorts rows within a part to accelerate range filters and allow primary key index jumps."),
        ("medium", "Explain the MergeTree engine and how it stores data.",
         "Each insert writes a small immutable part, and background merges combine parts to keep few, sorted, non-duplicated files. A primary key index built on the sort key allows skipping granules, and skipping indexes speed up sparse filters."),
        ("medium", "How do you optimize a slow ClickHouse query?",
         "Use EXPLAIN indexes=1 and profiling to find which partitions and granules are read, then add or align the ORDER BY key with the filter. Avoid SELECT *, use LowCardinality and codecs, pre-aggregate with materialized views, and cap max_threads to avoid oversubscription."),
        ("hard", "How do you choose between ReplacingMergeTree, SummingMergeTree, and AggregatingMergeTree?",
         "ReplacingMergeTree keeps the row with the highest version per ORDER BY key, deduping updates. SummingMergeTree pre-aggregates numeric columns, and AggregatingMergeTree stores aggregate states letting you finalize with -Merge combinators. Choose based on whether you need dedup, sums, or full aggregate functions like uniq and quantiles."),
    ],
    "Elasticsearch": [
        ("easy", "What is the inverted index and why is it fast?",
         "Elasticsearch maps each term to a sorted list of document IDs containing it, so term lookups skip scanning documents. Because the term dictionary is sorted and compressed, lookups are near constant time."),
        ("easy", "What is a mapping and why does the type matter?",
         "A mapping defines fields and their types, like text with analyzers or keyword for exact values. Choosing keyword for ids and text for prose enables correct search and aggregations."),
        ("medium", "What is an analyzer and how does the text/keyword difference affect search?",
         "An analyzer tokenizes and normalizes text into terms for the inverted index, with standard, custom, and other options. text fields are analyzed and not aggregatable, while keyword fields are stored as one term, so multi-fields cover both needs."),
        ("medium", "Explain the match, term, and phrase queries and when each applies.",
         "match analyzes the query text and scores against analyzed fields, term does exact lookup on keyword fields without analysis, and phrase checks adjacency with slop. Using term on a text field returns wrong results because the field is analyzed."),
        ("hard", "How do you design a shard and index strategy and recover from performance problems?",
         "Choose shard count from data volume and write throughput, not convenience, and avoid oversharding because each shard costs heap and file handles. Use rollover with ILM, time-based indices for retention, and routing for tenant affinity. Fix slow search with the profile API, mapping tuning, and forcing merges on read-only indices."),
    ],
    "Cassandra": [
        ("easy", "What is the CAP theorem and how does Cassandra choose its trade-offs?",
         "You can only pick two of consistency, availability, and partition tolerance under network partition. Cassandra prioritizes availability and partition tolerance with tunable eventual consistency per query."),
        ("easy", "What is a partition key and why is its choice critical?",
         "The partition key determines data distribution and is the only place queries can be routed. A low-cardinality or hot partition key creates a single shard hotspot, so distribute with hashers or time buckets."),
        ("medium", "Explain Cassandra's write path and hinted handoff.",
         "A write goes to a commit log plus memtable, is flushed to an SSTable, then compacted, and replication factors receive local copies. If a replica is down, a hint is stored elsewhere and replayed when it returns."),
        ("medium", "What are read repair and tombstone compaction?",
         "Read repair reconciles divergent replica data during a read, resolving inconsistency after the fact. Tombstones are markers for deletes, and too many create a performance problem, so aggressive tombstone compaction is important."),
        ("hard", "How do you diagnose a slow or hot Cassandra query and fix it?",
         "Look for large partition scans with nodetool getpartitionhistograms and cqlsh tracing, and check tombstones and partition sizes. Fix by adding or changing the clustering key to enable ORDER BY, bucketing large partitions, adjusting consistency levels, and using the read repair or hinted handoff diagnostics to restore replica health."),
    ],
    "Oracle Database": [
        ("easy", "What are the main differences between Oracle and other enterprise RDBMSs?",
         "Oracle uses pluggable datafiles, advanced partitioning and RAC for high availability, and PL/SQL. It is licensed per user or processor, which is its main cost difference from PostgreSQL or MySQL."),
        ("easy", "What is a PL/SQL block structure?",
         "A PL/SQL block has a declarative section, an executable section of statements, and an exception section for error handling. Anonymous blocks run once and stored procedures are compiled objects called by name."),
        ("medium", "Explain Oracle's undo, redo, and tablespace architecture.",
         "Redo logs record changes for crash recovery and are archived, undo tablespaces support rollback and consistent reads. Data lives in tablespaces, and the buffer cache and SGA manage memory in memory."),
        ("medium", "What is the difference between a sequence, identity column, and rowid in Oracle?",
         "A sequence generates numbers independently of transactions and may have gaps. A generated always as identity column ties the value to the table and can be default or override. Rowid is the physical location, which changes after row movement, so do not persist it."),
        ("hard", "How do you diagnose and improve Oracle performance with AWR and execution plans?",
         "Compare AWR reports over time to find top SQL by elapsed time and waits, then read the plan for full scans, bad cardinality estimates, and missing indexes. Fix with bind variable peeking, statistics histograms, partitioning, and index tuning, and address waits such as GC buffer busy or enq: TX by sizing SGA and shortening transactions."),
    ],
    "Microsoft SQL Server": [
        ("easy", "What are the recovery models in SQL Server?",
         "Simple allows log truncation after checkpoints, Full logs every operation for point-in-time restore, and Bulk-Logged optimizes bulk imports. Choose Full for high-value databases needing point-in-time recovery."),
        ("easy", "What is a primary key, foreign key, and index?",
         "A primary key uniquely identifies rows, a foreign key enforces referential integrity between tables, and an index speeds lookups at the cost of write overhead. Every table should have a clustered primary key."),
        ("medium", "Explain execution plans and how to read them for a slow query?",
         "SQL Server chooses a plan based on statistics, showing scans vs seeks, join types, and estimated versus actual rows. A large estimate mismatch means stale statistics, so update with UPDATE STATISTICS and inspect the plan with actual execution."),
        ("medium", "What are CTEs, window functions, and how do they differ from subqueries?",
         "A CTE is a named result set that can be referenced multiple times, while a subquery is inline. Window functions like ROW_NUMBER, SUM OVER, and LAG compute across a result set partition without collapsing rows."),
        ("hard", "How do you troubleshoot blocking and deadlocks in SQL Server?",
         "Use sp_blitzlock, sys.dm_exec_requests, and Extended Events to capture the blocking chain and lock resources. Fix with short transactions, consistent access order, proper indexes to avoid scans, READ_COMMITTED_SNAPSHOT for readers, and retry logic for the victim transaction."),
    ],
}
